## glibc Chunk Layout Reference (x86_64, glibc ≥ 2.27)

```
 chunk_start (= malloc_ptr - 16):
   [0..7]    prev_size  (only valid when prev chunk is free; otherwise = last 8B of prev userdata)
   [8..15]   size       (chunk_size | flags; bit0=PREV_INUSE, bit1=IS_MMAPPED, bit2=NON_MAIN_ARENA)
 malloc_ptr (= what malloc() returns):
   [0..N-1]  user data  (N = chunk_size - 8)  ← malloc_usable_size() may return N > requested!

 For malloc(1644) on x86_64:
   chunk_size = (1644 + 8 + 15) & ~15 = 1664
   usable     = 1664 - 8 = 1656 bytes
   padding    = 1656 - 1644 = 12 bytes  ← in the usable area, overlaps next chunk's prev_size!
   next chunk header starts at: malloc_ptr + 1648 (prev_size) and +1656 (size)
```

Key insight: `malloc_usable_size(p)` returns 1656 ≠ requested 1644. The 12 "extra" bytes
include the next chunk's `prev_size` field (bytes 1648..1655 of our region). Writing there
is detectable by ASAN (which tracks the REQUESTED size, not usable size), but glibc itself
will overwrite this with the correct value in `set_foot()` when our chunk is freed.

